Aberdeen City Council requires an integrated Destination Marketing, Development and Convention Bureau Service to increase awareness of Aberdeen, develop visitor demand and convert interest into leisure visits, business events, incentive travel, overnight stays, longer stays and increased visitor value.
The successful contractor will be required to provide:
integrated service leadership, governance and annual delivery planning;
development and application of a distinctive Aberdeen visitor proposition;
destination marketing, communications, digital content and public relations;
promotion of Council-approved events, venues, exhibitions and Events 365 priorities;
destination and visitor-product development;
travel-trade engagement, itinerary development, sales activity and familiarisation visits;
an Aberdeen Welcome service, including management of visitor-welcome volunteers;
structured engagement with accommodation providers, venues, visitor attractions, cultural and sporting organisations, event organisers, hospitality, retail, transport, universities and other visitor-economy partners;
a dedicated Aberdeen Convention Bureau function, including lead generation, opportunity qualification, ambassador development, bid preparation, site inspections, pipeline management and confirmed-event support;
administration of a ring-fenced Convention Bureau Subvention Fund within Council-approved criteria, budgets, governance and delegated authority;
consumer, visitor, delegate, business and campaign research;
evaluation, data management, performance reporting and evidence supporting transparent reporting on Visitor Levy expenditure; and
management and transfer of agreed digital assets, content, research, records and other Council-funded resources.
The maximum estimated Contract value is 6.10m GBP excluding VAT over the full potential five-year term. This is based on approved annual funding of up to 1.22m GBP.
The maximum value includes a ring-fenced Convention Bureau Subvention Fund. Tenderers must propose and justify the amount and proportion of the overall annual budget to be allocated to that Fund. The allocation must be identified separately from:
the cost of delivering the Convention Bureau service;
the cost of administering the Fund;
contractor fees and overheads; and
other campaign, programme and third-party expenditure.
The proposed allocation will inform the initial annual delivery plan but will remain subject to Council approval. A higher proposed Subvention Fund allocation will not, by itself, attract a higher evaluation score.
Uncommitted or unspent Subvention Funding will remain under the Council’s control and will not form part of the contractor’s remuneration.
The Council will retain strategic oversight and approval of annual delivery plans, workstream budgets and Subvention Fund allocations. Additional information: Prices are fixed for 24 months. Thereafter, annual increases to eligible recurring core-service charges may be requested, capped at the lower of CPI or 3%. No automatic increase applies. Reviews exclude Subvention Funding, mobilisation, campaign and programme budgets, third-party and pass-through costs, and expenditure reimbursed at cost.

